# Sourcing pronunciation audio

The speaker button plays a clip we **self-host** in
`public/audio/words/<id>.mp3`, wired via optional `audio` on a `SpokenWord` in
`src/lib/words/registry.ts`. Writing rules for *when* to register a loanword, and the periodic scan,
live in `.cursor/rules/loanword-speakers.mdc` (house style:
`.cursor/rules/history-articles.mdc`). **This file is the sourcing
catalog** — where to look per language, what we already have, what we ruled
out. **Update it whenever you add a language, find a clip, or rule a source
out**, so the next agent doesn't repeat a dead search.

## Hard rule — human recordings only

**A real human saying the matching word, or no speaker at all.**

- Never synthesize speech. No `window.speechSynthesis`, no cloud TTS, no
  generated voice, no "close enough" phonetic stand-in (German for Yiddish,
  etc.). A word with no verified clip ships with **no `audio` field** → the UI
  shows the native spelling and **no speaker icon**. That silence is correct.
- Verify **all three** before adding a clip: (1) it is an actual human voice,
  (2) it is the **matching word and sense** (Spanish *hora* “hour” ≠ the
  Romanian dance; Occitan *bulgar* ≠ the klezmer dance), (3) it is **openly
  licensed** for redistribution (public domain, CC0, CC BY, CC BY-SA). If any
  of the three is unproven, **do not add it**.
- **Never fabricate a URL, speaker, or license.** If you can't find and verify
  a clip, leave `audio` off and say so. Don't hotlink a source we haven't
  copied + attributed.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — TTS fallback, invented file, or wrong-sense Commons clip
audio: { src: "/audio/words/hora.mp3" }  // Spanish "hour"
utterance.text = "krechtz"; speechSynthesis.speak(utterance);

// ✅ GOOD — verified human clip, self-hosted, credited
audio: JEL("krechtz.mp3", "2481")
// ✅ ALSO GOOD — no clip found → omit `audio`, no speaker
```

## Languages we currently know

Every `native.language` / `lang` in `WORDS` must have a row here. When you
register a word in a **new** language, add the row in the same change
(Wikidata QID + best sources + any gotchas).

| Language | BCP-47 | Lingua Libre QID | Best sources (try in this order) | Gotchas |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Yiddish** | `yi` | `Q8641` | **JEL**; then Commons `LL-Q8641`; yi.wiktionary | JEL is the strongest for klezmer/Jewish terms. Lingua Libre Yiddish is sparse (none of our words as of 2026-09-05). |
| **Hebrew** | `he-IL` | `Q9288` | **JEL**; then Commons `LL-Q9288`; he.wiktionary | Prayer/mode names (*Ahava Rabbah*) often live on JEL, not Hebrew Wiktionary. |
| **Romanian** | `ro-RO` | `Q7913` | Commons `LL-Q7913`; ro.wiktionary; Commons filename search | Dance terms (*doina*, *hora*, *sârbă*) often have only the common-noun / other-language sense recorded. **Do not attach the wrong sense.** |
| **Greek** | `el` | `Q9129` | Commons `LL-Q9129`; el.wiktionary | *Misirlou* (Μισιρλού) is a song title / proper name — Lingua Libre is unlikely. Don’t attach a random “Egypt” clip. |
| **Polish** | `pl` | `Q809` | Commons `LL-Q809`; Commons `Pl-<word>.ogg`; pl.wiktionary | Latin script, so there's no native parenthetical — the value is the *speaker*. Lingua Libre Polish is large. `oj-dana.ogg` (refrain vocable) shipped from `File:Pl-dana.ogg`, CC BY-SA 3.0. |

English (`en`, `Q1860`) is not a registry language, but Lingua Libre English
sometimes has a human saying a loanword that has entered English (e.g.
*klezmer*). Prefer a recording in the home language when one exists.

